      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{5}\\}=\\frac{5!}{{s}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\} & =\\frac{5!}{(s-7)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
